{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.definition_globs`.\n\n**Matching rule**\n- `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` tests the real `validate` and `matches` functions.\n  - Whole-name matching: prefix and suffix variants are rejected.\n  - `*` crossing dots: `foo.*.bar` matches both `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`.\n  - Literal characters: `.` is literal (`foo.bar` does not match `fooxbar`), and `_` and `-` are literal.\n  - A trailing literal dot: `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`.\n  - `*` matching zero characters is covered end to end. The CLI test `commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors` in `tests/commands.rs` has `requirements.*ready` selecting both `requirements.ready` and `requirements.xready`.\n- Invalid globs: `validate(\"\")` and `validate(\"foo[bar]\")` are both asserted to be errors.\n\n**Every selection path**\n- Requirements evidence inputs: `requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order` in `src/evidence.rs` covers these cases.\n  - `foo.*.bar` matches both an example and a requirement across dots.\n  - Added, removed and changed matches are detected.\n  - A glob that matches nothing is reported.\n  - `foo.[a].bar` and the empty glob are rejected as invalid.\n- CLI definition filters: `commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors` in `tests/commands.rs` covers `status` and `info`.\n  - `[` is rejected with \"invalid definition glob\".\n  - An unmatched selector is rejected.\n  - A wildcard selects both items.\n  - `verbose_status_lists_fixme_judgments_and_globs_scope_issue_groups` and `status_retains_repository_findings_without_indexing_unreferenced_functions` also apply selections to `status`.\n- Authoring `references` tool: `domain_discovery_returns_readable_nodes_without_recording_their_contents` in `src/authoring/domain_tools.rs` covers `LookupRequirements`.\n  - `requirements.s*` selects exactly the example `requirements.specific`.\n  - `[bad]` yields an \"invalid definition glob\" error.\n  - A selection of `None` returns everything.\n  - The empty glob is not tested through the CLI or the authoring tool. Both use the same `validate`, which is tested directly and through evidence inputs, so I don't count this as a gap.\n\n**Requirements and named examples, but not named definitions**\n- Examples are selected in the evidence test (`foo.a.b.bar`), the CLI test (`rules.case`) and the authoring test (`specific`).\n- `selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions` in `tests/definitions.rs` shows that `rules.*` selects the requirement and the example but not the `rules.used` definition. It also shows that an exact definition name selects nothing, for evidence inputs and for the CLI `status` and `info` commands.\n\nI found no gap and no marking gap.",
  "references": "d09c1fa492d8c1d0cd091d60248c75aedc90ed2fe8e9f901f1aa0891800557d2",
